{"qid":"Q332005","names":["Principality of Achaea"],"title":"Principality of Achaea","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Principality of Achaea (also Morea) was a Crusader state in the Peloponnese, established after the Fourth Crusade (1204) by William of Champlitte and Geoffrey I of Villehardouin from territories of the dismembered Byzantine Empire. Under Geoffrey II (r. 1229-1246) and William II of Villehardouin (r. 1246-1278) it became the wealthiest Frankish state in Greece, with its court at Andravida; William II's defeat and capture at the Battle of Pelagonia (1259) forced him to cede Mystras and other Laconian fortresses to the restored Byzantine Empire under Michael VIII Palaiologos, seeding the rival Byzantine Despotate of the Morea. After passing through Angevin, Navarrese, and Florentine (Acciaioli) hands, the principality was progressively swallowed by the Despotate: Constantine and Thomas Palaiologos conquered its heartland and, on the death of the last prince Centurione II Zaccaria in 1432, his remaining lands were absorbed by the Byzantines, ending the Frankish principality.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The principality's core Peloponnesian territory is well-documented through Crusader chronicles and Byzantine sources; exact inland boundaries fluctuated and are approximately reconstructed.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Principality of Achaea","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"William I of Champlitte","nativeName":"Guillaume Ier de Champlitte","roman":"","start":1205,"end":1209,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Geoff